  "quickStartTitle": "Get started",
  "step1Title": "Connect the adapter",
  "step1Body": "Plug in the USB–Serial adapter, then pick its port in Bridge setup.",
  "step2Title": "Start the bridge",
  "step2Body": "Povad opens the port and waits for one TCP client.",
  "step2BleBody": "Povad opens the serial port and advertises a Bluetooth LE service for one client.",
  "step3Title": "Connect your client",
  "step3Body": "Point your TCP software at the address shown on Overview. Bytes pass through unchanged.",
  "step3BleBody": "Scan for the advertising name in your BLE client. Use the service and characteristic UUIDs from Bridge setup. Subscribe to notifications and send writes. Bytes pass through unchanged.",
  "step3BleWindowsBody": "Scan for this computer’s Bluetooth name, or search for the service UUID from Bridge setup. Subscribe to notifications and send writes. Bytes pass through unchanged.",
  "faqTitle": "Questions and fixes",
  "faqClientQ": "My TCP client cannot connect",
  "faqClientA": "Check that the status reads “Waiting for a client” and use the address from Overview. From another computer, turn on Allow LAN clients and use a LAN address. A firewall can also block the port.",
  "faqBleClientQ": "My BLE client cannot connect",
  "faqBleClientA": "Check that Povad is waiting for a client, Bluetooth is on, and your client scans for Bluetooth LE devices. Use the advertising name and UUIDs from Bridge setup. If Require pairing is on, pair when your system asks. BT24 clients that never pair need Require pairing off.",
  "faqBleWindowsClientA": "Check that Povad is waiting for a client and Bluetooth is on. Windows advertises under this computer’s Bluetooth name; you can also search for the service UUID from Bridge setup. If Require pairing is on, pair when asked. BT24 clients that never pair need Require pairing off.",
  "faqPortQ": "The serial port cannot be opened",
  "faqPortA": "Close other apps that use the port, reconnect the adapter, and check the port name. On Linux, add your user to the dialout group. On Windows, find the COM number in Device Manager.",
  "faqEndedQ": "Why did the session end?",
  "faqEndedA": "Each session serves one TCP client. When it disconnects, the session ends so no bytes reach the wrong client. Select Start again to wait for the next one.",
  "faqEndedBleA": "Each session serves one BLE client. When it disconnects, the session ends. Select Start again to advertise for the next client.",
  "faqBytesQ": "Does Povad change my data?",
  "faqBytesA": "No. Povad relays bytes in both directions without parsing a protocol, changing checksums, polling the device, or replying on its behalf.",
  "faqLanQ": "Is it safe to allow LAN clients?",
  "faqLanA": "LAN clients are not authenticated and can send bytes to your device. Allow them only on a network you trust, and turn LAN access off when you finish.",
  "faqBlePairingQ": "Should my BLE client pair?",
  "faqBlePairingA": "Require pairing is on by default. Pair with Povad when your system asks. The connection is encrypted; another client cannot take over the active bridge.",
  "faqBleOpenA": "Require pairing is off. This allows BT24-style clients that do not pair, but any nearby Bluetooth device can connect and send bytes to your serial device. Use this mode only where you trust devices in range.",
  "faqPrivacyQ": "Does Povad send my data anywhere?",
  "faqPrivacyA": "No. Serial data stays between your device and your client. Povad only asks GitHub whether a newer release exists.",
